# Error handling and logging

Treat every external system (GitHub API, Firestore, filesystem, network) and every
async operation as something that can fail. Prefer preventing errors over handling
them, fail fast on invalid state, and never trade correctness for convenience.
Messages, logs, and comments are in English.

## Rule 1 — Throw `Error`, never strings or plain objects

Only `Error` (or a subclass) carries a stack trace.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
throw `not found`;
throw { code: 404 };

// ✅ GOOD
throw new Error('Repository tree not found');
```

## Rule 2 — Messages are actionable: subject + context

State what failed and include the identifiers needed to act on it (repo, ref,
path, status, count). Avoid bare "Error", "Failed", "Something went wrong".

```ts
// ❌ BAD — no context
throw new Error('request failed');
console.log('done');

// ✅ GOOD
throw new Error(`GitHub API ${response.status} for ${owner}/${repo}`);
console.log(`Created ${createdFiles} file(s) and ${createdDirs} dir(s)`);
```

## Rule 3 — Preserve the cause when wrapping

When an error crosses a layer boundary, enrich it with context and keep the
original via `{ cause }`. Never flatten it into a string or drop it.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — original stack lost
catch (error) {
  throw new Error('Failed to fetch blob');
}

// ✅ GOOD
catch (error) {
  throw new Error(`Failed to fetch blob ${sha}`, { cause: error });
}
```

## Rule 4 — Catch only when you add value

Catch when you can recover, add context, translate to a domain error, clean up,
or log at the boundary. A `try/catch` that only rethrows is noise.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
try {
  return await service();
} catch (error) {
  throw error;
}

// ✅ GOOD
return service();
```

## Rule 5 — Never swallow errors

No empty `catch`. Handle it, rethrow it (optionally wrapped), or log it with
context — silent failures hide bugs.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
try {
  await pull(options);
} catch {}

// ✅ GOOD
try {
  await pull(options);
} catch (error) {
  console.error(`Pull failed for ${options.repoUrl}:`, error);
  throw error;
}
```

## Rule 6 — Log once, at the boundary

Each failure is logged exactly once, at the outermost layer that owns the
request: the HTTP handler in `functions/` (via `logError`), the CLI entrypoint,
or the client hook that renders the error. Lower layers throw; they do not log.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — store logs, domain logs, handler logs the same failure
async function add(data: Repository) {
  try {
    return await collection.add(data);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error); // and again two layers up
    throw error;
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD — only the handler logs; the store and domain just throw
export const apiStoreRepoInfo = onRequest(async (request, response) => {
  try {
    const id = await storeRepoInfo(request.body);
    response.status(201).send({ id });
  } catch (error) {
    logError('Failed to write repository', error);
    response.status(400).send({ error: 'Invalid repository payload' });
  }
});
```

## Rule 7 — Right level, proportionate volume

`console.error` / `logger.error` for failures, `warn` for recoverable or
tolerated conditions, `log` / `info` for normal progress. Never report an error
at `log` level. Summarize bulk work with a count instead of one line per item in
a hot loop.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — error reported at log level
console.log('failed to write file');

// ✅ GOOD
console.error(`Failed to write ${target}`);
console.warn(`Directory "${prefix}" is truncated — result may be partial`);
console.log(`Repository: ${owner}/${repo} (ref: ${resolvedRef})`);
```

## Rule 8 — No secrets or personal data

Tokens, passwords, keys, and personal data never go into an error message or
log — they leak into stack traces and log aggregation.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
console.log(`Authorizing with token ${token}`);
throw new Error(`Auth failed for token ${token}`);

// ✅ GOOD
console.log('Authorizing with provided token');
throw new Error('Authentication failed (401)');
```

## Rule 9 — Validate early, fail fast

Validate public and untrusted input (API payloads, CLI arguments, env vars,
config) at the boundary with a schema, then stop immediately on invalid state.
Never rely on a downstream failure to do the validation. Schema conventions:
`typescript-best-practices.mdc`.

## Rule 10 — Domain errors for business failures

When a caller must branch on the failure kind (validation vs not-found vs
conflict vs permission), throw a specific `Error` subclass rather than a generic
`Error` with a magic message.

## Rule 11 — Promises and concurrency

Never leave a rejected promise unhandled and never use the `void` operator to
discard one. Prefer `await`; for intentional fire-and-forget, attach `.catch()`
and log with context. Use `Promise.all` only when one failure should fail the
whole operation, `Promise.allSettled` when partial success is acceptable.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
void doWork();

// ✅ GOOD
await doWork();

doWork().catch((error: unknown) => {
  console.error('Failed to do work:', error);
});
```

## Rule 12 — Timeouts and cleanup

Every network call has an explicit timeout; never wait indefinitely. Release
resources in `finally`, via `AbortController`, or with disposable resources.

## Rule 13 — User-facing errors hide internals

Show the user a clear, actionable, human-readable message. Log the technical
details separately at the boundary (Rule 6).

```ts
// ❌ BAD — stack trace and internal path reach the UI
setError(String(error));

// ✅ GOOD
console.error(`Failed to load repositories for ${userId}:`, error);
setError('Could not load repositories. Try again later.');
```

## Checklist

- Throw `Error` subclasses only; messages name the subject and carry identifiers.
- Wrapped errors keep `{ cause }`; no rethrow-only `try/catch`; no empty `catch`.
- One log per failure, at the boundary; lower layers throw.
- `error` / `warn` / `log` match intent; no secrets or personal data in messages.
- Validate at the boundary and fail fast; domain errors where callers branch.
- No `void` on promises; `.catch()` with context for fire-and-forget; `all` vs `allSettled` chosen deliberately.
- Network calls have timeouts; resources are released in `finally` / `AbortController`.
- Users see actionable copy, never internals.
